      "desc": "Facilities, general.",
      "narrative": "An enclosure for Marabou stork did not contain one of the storks securely. A female Marabou stork was reported by a\nfacility representative to the USDA on May 5, 2026 as having been located out of the facility in Fond du Lac, WI\n(approximately 70 mi in a straight line from the primary enclosure). Based on a known foot abnormality, the facility is\nconfident that this is a stork that left her primary enclosure in the 2025 exhibit season (even though wings had been\nclipped) and after regular sightings which included care for it within the perimeter and attempts to safely lure it to a\ncapture area, sightings stopped until May 2026 when new sightings occurred in Fond du Lac, WI. The representative\nreported being in contact with people that had spotted her and to contact facility representatives if spotted again so that a\nplan for re-capture could be put in place. A public member that captured her reportedly contacted a different facility who\noffered to house it on May 14, 2026 and then was later returned to this facility. The stork appears to have been re-\ncaptured without incident, is in good body condition and has normal behavior, but not having had an enclosure that can\nsafely contain her put her at risk for injury. Ensure to maintain enclosures in a manner that securely contains the birds.\n***Maintain correct from July 1, 2026 forward.\n***Incident was reported to USDA on May 5, 2026 and facility has moved Marabou storks to enclosures that do not have\nopen tops in which they could fly out.\nThis inspection and exit interview were conducted with facility representatives.\nn"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": "3.150(a) \n   \nFacilities, general. \nAn enclosure for Marabou stork did not contain one of the storks securely. A female Marabou stork was reported by a \nfacility representative to the USDA on May 5, 2026 as having been located out of the facility in Fond du Lac, WI \n(approximately 70 mi in a straight line from the primary enclosure). Based on a known foot abnormality, the facility is \nconfident that this is a stork that left her primary enclosure in the 2025 exhibit season (even though wings had been \nclipped) and after regular sightings which included care for it within the perimeter and attempts to safely lure it to a \ncapture area, sightings stopped until May 2026 when new sightings occurred in Fond du Lac, WI. The representative \nreported being in contact with people that had spotted her and to contact facility representatives if spotted again so that a \nplan for re-capture could be put in place. A public member that captured her reportedly contacted a different facility who \noffered to house it on May 14, 2026 and then was later returned to this facility. The stork appears to have been re-\ncaptured without incident, is in good body condition and has normal behavior, but not having had an enclosure that can \nsafely contain her put her at risk for injury. Ensure to maintain enclosures in a manner that securely contains the birds.  \n***Maintain correct from July 1, 2026 forward.  \n***Incident was reported to USDA on May 5, 2026 and facility has moved Marabou storks to enclosures that do not have \nopen tops in which they could fly out. \n \nThis inspection and exit interview were conducted with facility representatives. \n \n n",
